## [Button: Article ]

## Abstract

Let 𝐺 be a simple graph with adjacency matrix 𝐴⁡(𝐺) , degree diagonal matrix 𝐷⁡(𝐺), and let 𝑙⁡(𝐺) be the line graph of 𝐺 . In 2017, Nikiforov defined the 𝐴𝛼 -matrix of 𝐺 , 𝐴𝛼⁡(𝐺) , as a linear convex combination of 𝐴⁡(𝐺) and 𝐷⁡(𝐺) , in the following way, 𝐴𝛼⁡(𝐺) ≔𝛼⁢𝐴⁡(𝐺) +(1−𝛼)⁢𝐷⁡(𝐺), where 𝛼 ∈[0,1] . In this study, we present some bounds for the largest eigenvalue of 𝐴𝛼⁡(𝐺), and for some eigenvalues of 𝐴𝛼⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺)) . Extremal graphs attaining some of these bounds are also characterized. Furthermore, some comparisons between the new bounds obtained in this study, and between these and some bounds presented by Nikiforov are made.

Keywords: line graphs; characteristic polynomial; A α -eigenvalues; A α -matrix

### 3.3 Some results for 𝐴𝛼 -spectra of line graphs

In this section, we present some results involving line graphs. The study of the 𝐴𝛼 matrix is very recent and, after a bibliographic research, no results were found, at least so far, involving line graphs and this matrix. Next theorem is based on Theorem 2.12 and presents a lower bound for the smallest eigenvalue of 𝐴𝛼⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺)) .

### Theorem 3.7

Let G be a graph with m edges and 𝛿⁡(𝐺) its minimum degree. Then,

(20) 𝜆𝑚⁡(𝐴𝛼⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺)))≥2⁢𝛼⁢𝛿⁡(𝐺)−2,

for 𝛼 ∈[0,1) .

### Proof

Let 𝐵 be the incidence matrix of 𝐺 . From Remark 2.16, we have

(1−𝛼)⁢𝐵𝑇⁢𝐵=𝐴𝛼⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺))+2⁢(1−𝛼)⁢𝐼𝑚−𝛼⁢𝐷⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺)).

Let {𝑤1,…,𝑤𝑚} be the orthonormal basis of eigenvectors associated with 𝜆1⁡(𝐴𝛼⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺))),…,𝜆𝑚⁡(𝐴𝛼⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺))) such that 𝐴𝛼⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺))⁢𝑤𝑖 =𝜆𝑖⁡(𝐴𝛼⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺)))⁢𝑤𝑖, ∀𝑖 =1,…,𝑚 . Then,

(1−𝛼)⁢𝑤𝑇𝑚⁢𝐵𝑇⁢𝐵⁢𝑤𝑚=𝑤𝑇𝑚⁢𝐴𝛼⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺))⁢𝑤𝑚+2⁢(1−𝛼)⁢𝑤𝑇𝑚⁢𝑤𝑚−𝛼⁢𝑤𝑇𝑚⁢𝐷⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺))⁢𝑤𝑚≤𝜆𝑚⁡(𝐴𝛼⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺)))+2⁢(1−𝛼)−𝛼⁢𝛿⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺)).

As 𝐵𝑇⁢𝐵 is positive semi-definite, we obtain

0≤𝑤𝑇𝑚⁢𝐵𝑇⁢𝐵⁢𝑤𝑚≤𝜆𝑚⁡(𝐴𝛼⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺)))+2⁢(1−𝛼)−𝛼⁢𝛿⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺))1−𝛼.

Therefore,

0≤𝜆𝑚⁡(𝐴𝛼⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺)))+2⁢(1−𝛼)−𝛼⁢𝛿⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺)).

From Theorem 2.11, the result follows

□ 𝜆𝑚⁡(𝐴𝛼⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺)))≥2⁢𝛼⁢𝛿⁡(𝐺)−2.

### Corollary 3.8

If G is a r-regular graph with n vertices, 𝑚 ≠0 edges, 𝑟 ≥2 , and 𝛼 ∈[0,1) , then 𝜆𝑚⁡(𝐴𝛼⁡(𝑙⁡(𝐺))) =2⁢𝑟⁢𝛼 −2 if and only if 𝑚 >𝑛 or G is bipartite.

